{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"cap-v2-stateful_workflows-javascript-repair-02\",\"problem\":\"Repair the CommonJS module below. It must export exactly the `createBatch` function with `module.exports`.\\n\\n`createBatch(initialItems)` receives an array of strings and returns an isolated batch with four methods: `stage(operationId, item)`, `commit(operationId)`, `rollback(operationId)`, and `snapshot()`.\\n\\nThe batch begins in phase `OPEN`, with a copy of `initialItems` as its committed items and no pending items. While open, `stage` appends a pending string. `commit` moves every pending item to the committed list and enters `COMMITTED`. `rollback` discards every pending item and enters `ROLLED_BACK`. After either terminal transition, new operations must throw `Error(\\\"Batch is closed\\\")`.\\n\\nOperation IDs are unique across all three mutating methods. Repeating an already processed ID must be an idempotent no-op that returns the current snapshot, even after the batch has closed.\\n\\nEvery method returns a snapshot shaped as `{ phase, committed, pending }`. Each snapshot must contain fresh arrays: mutating the input array, a returned snapshot, or arrays from an older snapshot must never change the batch's internal state.\\n\\nUse only standard JavaScript and do not access the filesystem, network, clock, or randomness.\\n\\nKnown-bad source to repair:\\n```javascript\\nfunction createBatch(initialItems) {\\n  let phase = 'OPEN';\\n  let committed = initialItems.slice();\\n  let pending = [];\\n  const processed = new Set();\\n\\n  function snapshot() {\\n    return {\\n      phase,\\n      committed: committed.slice(),\\n      pending: pending\\n    };\\n  }\\n\\n  function ensureOpen() {\\n    if (phase !== 'OPEN') {\\n      throw new Error('Batch is closed');\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function stage(operationId, item) {\\n    if (processed.has(operationId)) {\\n      return snapshot();\\n    }\\n